Running GNUWorld with podman (rootless)
=======================================

Install and configure podman
----------------------------
1. Install podman:

    sudo apt install podman podman-compose uidmap passt

2. mkdir -p ~/.config/containers/

3. Tell podman where to resolve unqualified packages:

    cat > ~/.config/containers/registries.conf <<'EOF'
unqualified-search-registries = ["docker.io"]
EOF

2. Verify:

    podman run hello-world


Building
--------
1. Copy the example config files:

    cp bin/GNUWorld.example.conf bin/GNUWorld.conf
    cp bin/cservice.example.conf bin/cservice.conf
    cp bin/ccontrol.example.conf bin/ccontrol.conf
    ... etc.

2. Edit bin/GNUWorld.conf, at minimum, set the uplink address, port,
   password, and server name.

3. Edit bin/cservice.conf and bin/ccontrol.conf (et al). Set the database
   connection to match the values in docker-compose.yml:

4. Build:

    podman compose build

   Note that only cservice and ccontrol are built by default. See the Modules
   section below.


Running as a user systemd unit
------------------------------
1. sudo loginctl enable-linger jotun
2. mkdir -p ~/.config/systemd/user/
3. Create the systemd user unit:

    cat > ~/.config/systemd/user/gnuworld.service <<'EOF'

[Unit]
Description=gnuworld compose stack
After=network-online.target

[Service]
Type=oneshot
RemainAfterExit=yes
WorkingDirectory=/home/jotun/gnuworld
ExecStart=/usr/bin/podman compose up --detach
ExecStop=/usr/bin/podman compose down

[Install]
WantedBy=default.target
EOF

4. systemctl --user daemon-reload
5. systemctl --user enable --now gnuworld.service

   On first start the entrypoint creates and initializes the cservice
   and ccontrol databases automatically. Subsequent starts skip this step.

   To initialize other modules' databases, edit docker/entrypoint.sh.

5. Follow logs:

    podman compose logs -f gnuworld


Configuration
-------------
All bin/*.conf files are bind-mounted into the container read-only.
The entrypoint copies them into the working directory at each startup,
so any config change takes effect on the next restart, no rebuild needed:

    podman compose restart gnuworld

Changing source code requires a rebuild:

    podman compose up --build --detach

Note: schema file changes in the image have no effect if the databases
already exist.


Modules
-------
Only cservice and ccontrol are compiled by default. To change that, edit
Dockerfile. Which modules actually load is controlled by the "module =" lines
in bin/GNUWorld.conf. After modifying the config, a restart (not a rebuild) is
sufficient.

Each module that requires a database needs it initialized separately. The
entrypoint only handles cservice and ccontrol automatically. For other
modules, see the corresponding doc/README.* file. You can add them to
docker/entrypoint.sh or apply the schema manually:

    podman compose exec db psql -U postgres -d <dbname> -f /path/to/schema.sql


Data persistence
----------------
PostgreSQL data is stored in the pgdata named volume. To wipe the databases
and reinitialize from schema:

    podman compose down --volumes
    podman compose up --detach
